Title:
Basic Reversing of a Windows Kernel Driver: How WESP Talks to User Mode

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post walks through a “basic reversing” workflow to understand what an undocumented Windows kernel driver does and how it communicates with user mode, using WESP (Windows Endpoint Security Platform) as the target. It shows how to derive architecture and communication mechanisms without decompiling code—starting from the Services registry entry, then locating the driver object in the Object Manager namespace, and finally inspecting it with WinDbg. By comparing a “normal” driver (Process Explorer’s PROCEXP driver) to WESP via `!drvobj`, it highlights that WESP exposes no device objects and no IRP dispatch routines, implying it doesn’t use `CreateFile`/`DeviceIoControl`. Import analysis of `EspClient.dll` and `wesp.sys` reveals the real IPC path: a file system mini-filter using Filter Manager communication ports (`FLTLIB.DLL`, `FilterConnectCommunicationPort`, `FltCreateCommunicationPort`). The key takeaway is that imports + object namespace inspection can quickly identify a driver’s user-mode attack surface (e.g., the port name) before heavy static reversing. Useful for security researchers, red teamers, and driver developers analyzing new/unknown kernel components.

Technical Focus:
- Windows driver registration and load characteristics via `HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services` (`Start`, `Type`)
- Object Manager namespace enumeration (`\Driver`, `\FileSystem`) with Sysinternals WinObj
- WinDbg kernel debugging for driver introspection (`!drvobj`, `.reload`, `uf`)
- IRP dispatch model vs. mini-filter architecture (no `DEVICE_OBJECT` / no IRP handlers)
- Filter Manager communication ports (FltLib user-mode APIs, `FltCreateCommunicationPort`)
- PE import/string analysis to infer IPC mechanisms and object names

Use Cases:
- Rapid triage of unknown/undocumented Windows drivers to identify IPC and attack surface
- Determining whether a driver is a standard WDM driver vs. file system mini-filter
- Finding communication port names to build custom clients/fuzzers for message interfaces
- Validating how security/EDR-related components export events to user mode
- Building detection/monitoring around Filter Manager port connections and clients

Keywords:
WESP, wesp.sys, Windows Endpoint Security Platform, Windows 11 Insider, kernel driver reversing, HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services, Start=0 boot driver, Type=2 mini-filter, Object Manager namespace, \\FileSystem, WinObj, WinDbg, !drvobj, DRIVER_OBJECT, DEVICE_OBJECT, IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL, DeviceIoControl, Filter Manager, FLTLIB.DLL, FilterConnectCommunicationPort, FltCreateCommunicationPort, EspClient.dll, communication port, PE imports, string search